+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service, store or dependency for larder, or reviews such a proposal. It holds what the
+team favors when two good options conflict. Written in October 2026, when a proposal to add Elasticsearch for item
+search became the third time the team argued from the start whether larder should run one more service, after Redis
+for sessions in 2023 and Redis for Celery in March 2025.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Larder runs one database and one web app, and a new service must do what Postgres cannot, with two volunteers able to restore it
+
+Rules out running a service for a job Postgres can do, and running one that only one volunteer knows how to restore.
+It does not rule out every service: the card gateway runs outside larder, and a hosted service nobody on the team has
+to run passes.
+
+The volunteers who run larder are few and not always available. In 2023 Redis for sessions was turned down because
+only one volunteer would have run it. In March 2025 Redis did run, for Celery's queue: it evicted 300 receipt jobs
+one Saturday night, and the one volunteer who could restore it spent a day resending receipts by hand. Both times
+Postgres did the job. In October 2026 a volunteer proposed Elasticsearch because searching for "rice" took four
+seconds and missed "Rice, brown"; in a trial, Postgres full-text search on the item name answered in 40 ms and found
+it, and was taken instead.
+
+Applied in [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
